{#ch33-0001}
# Baldr’s Dreams

{#ch33-0002 ref="bdr.1"}
::: stanza
At once the Æsir[^3] were all in assembly,
and the Ásynjur [^4] all in consultation,
and the powerful deities deliberated about it,
why Baldr’s dreams were baleful.[^5]
:::

{#ch33-0003 ref="bdr.2"}
::: stanza
Up rose Óðinn, Gautr of ages,[^6]
and on Sleipnir he laid a saddle;
he rode from there down to Niflhel,[^7]
he met a whelp, the one which came from Hel.[^8]
:::

{#ch33-0004 ref="bdr.3"}
::: stanza
It was bloody across the front of its breast,[^9]
and it bayed long at the father of incantation;[^10]
forward rode Óðinn, the earth-way resounded,
he came to the high hall of Hel.[^11]
:::

{#ch33-0005 ref="bdr.4"}
::: stanza
Then Óðinn rode east of the door,[^12]
to where he knew a seeress’s grave \[to be\];
incantations for the slain he spoke to the one wise in witchcraft,[^13]
until she rose under duress, spoke a dead body’s words:
:::

{#ch33-0006 ref="bdr.5"}
::: stanza
‘What kind of man is it, unknown to me,
who has made me \[undertake\] an arduous journey?
I was snowed-in with snow and lashed with rain
and drenched with dew — long was I dead!’[^14]
:::

{#ch33-0007 ref="bdr.6"}
::: stanza
‘I am called Vegtamr,[^15] I am the son of Valtamr;[^16]
tell me news from Hel — I remember \[things\] from home[^17] —
for whom are the benches strewn with arm-rings,
the floor beautifully flooded with gold?’
:::

{#ch33-0008 ref="bdr.7"}
::: stanza
‘Here stands mead brewed for Baldr,
shining drinks — a shield lies over them —
and the sons of the Æsir[^18] \[are\] in great anxiety.
I spoke under duress, now I shall be silent!’
:::

{#ch33-0009 ref="bdr.8"}
::: stanza
‘Don’t be silent, seeress! I want to question you\!
Until all is known,[^19] I’ll want to know more\!
Who will be Baldr’s slayer
and rob Óðinn’s son of life?’
:::

{#ch33-0010 ref="bdr.9"}
::: stanza
‘Hǫðr will bear the high glory-tree[^20] there,[^21]
he will become Baldr’s slayer
and rob Óðinn’s son of life.
I spoke under duress, now I shall be silent!’
:::

{#ch33-0011 ref="bdr.10"}
::: stanza
‘Don’t be silent, seeress! I want to question you\!
Until all is known, I’ll want to know more\!
Who will avenge Hǫðr’s hateful deed
and bear Baldr’s slayer to the pyre?’
:::

{#ch33-0012 ref="bdr.11"}
::: stanza
‘Rindr will bear Váli [^22] in western halls,[^23]
that son of Óðinn will slay when one night old;
he won’t wash his hands or comb his hair
before he bears Baldr’s enemy to the pyre.
I spoke under duress, now I shall be silent!’
:::

{#ch33-0013 ref="bdr.12"}
::: stanza
‘Don’t be silent, seeress! I want to question you\!
Until all is known, I’ll want to know more\!
Who are the maidens who will weep for their loved one[^24]
and cast their neck’s corners[^25] to the heavens?’[^26]
:::

{#ch33-0014 ref="bdr.13"}
::: stanza
‘You’re not Vegtamr, as I thought,
rather you’re Óðinn, the old Gautr!’[^27]
‘You’re neither a seeress nor a wise woman,
rather you’re the mother of three giants!’[^28]
:::

{#ch33-0015 ref="bdr.14"}
::: stanza
‘Ride home, Óðinn, and be proud\![^29]
May no one come back again to visit me thus
when[^30] Loki slips loose from his bonds[^31]
and the doom of the powers comes tearing \[down\]!’
:::

[^3]: Gods\.

[^4]: Goddesses\.

[^5]: Cf\. {_Þrk._} 14.

[^6]: Or ‘Gautr of men’, {_Gautr_} being an alias of Óðinn. {_Gautr_} could also be a common noun meaning ‘sacrificial victim (from whom blood has poured)’ (cf. OE {_geotan_} ‘to pour, shed’) in reference to Óðinn’s sacrifice of himself ({_Háv._} 138–39) or of his eye ({_Vsp._} 28) to gain knowledge. Cf. {_aldinn Gautr_} ‘the old Gautr’ in {_BDr._} 13.

[^7]: ‘Mist/Darkness Hel’.

[^8]: With this hellhound, cf. Garmr in {_Vsp._} 43, the dogs in {_FSk._} 11 (and preceding prose) and {_Fj_}. 19–24, and Kerberos, the dog which guards the entrance to Hades in Greek mythology. Hel is here the Norse underworld of the dead.

[^9]: The blood is presumably that of dead people whom the dog has devoured for trying to leave Hel, or of living people who have attempted to enter Hel.

[^10]: Óðinn. Cf. Garmr’s howling in {_Vsp_}. The initial prose to {_Grm._} says that no dog will attack Óðinn.

[^11]: Here Hel is either the underworld or the goddess who presides over it.

[^12]: Presumably the door to Hel’s hall.

[^13]: Cf\. {_Háv._} 157.

[^14]: Cf\. {_HH. II_} 44.

[^15]: ‘Way Ready (One)’.

[^16]: ‘Slaughtered/Slain Accustomed (One)’.

[^17]: I.e., the world of the living.

[^18]: The gods themselves.

[^19]: Or perhaps ‘Until I’m all-knowing’.

[^20]: {_Hróðrbarmr_} is an obscure term. Possibly it describes the mistletoe with which Hǫðr kills Baldr in Icelandic tradition, or Baldr himself. The adjective {_hávan_} ‘high’ might reflect either mistletoe’s elevated position in a tree or Baldr’s stature. Cf. {_Vsp._} 31–32.

[^21]: Or ‘here’ (i.e., to Hel).

[^22]: This word is absent from {*A*}. The emendation raises the possibility of alliteration with an East Norse, or pre-literary West Norse, form of {_Rindr_}, namely \*{_Vrindr_}. For the story of Váli’s birth and avenging of Baldr, see {_Vsp._} 32-3.

[^23]: The gods’ halls were usually located in the west.

[^24]: I.e., perhaps, for Baldr. Alternatively, {_at muni_} might mean ‘to their hearts’ content’.

[^25]: Possibly a term for the lower parts of women’s head-dresses or for ships’ sails, here used {_pars pro toto_}.

[^26]: Óðinn’s question is obscure. The answer might be ‘waves’—elsewhere personified as daughters of Ægir—which will toss their white (metaphorical) hoods or Baldr’s funeral ship. Or perhaps, as with another question asked by Óðinn in {_Vm._} 54, the question is unanswerable, and it is this that betrays his identity. Óðinn asks a similar riddling question, to which the answer is ‘waves’, in the {_Gátur Gestumblindi_} ‘Riddles of Gestumblindi’, part of {_Hervarar saga ok Heiðreks_}. Cf. Óðinn’s question about the identity of ‘maidens who travel over the sea’ in {_Vm._} 48.

[^27]: Or {_gautr_} ‘sacrifice’. Cf. {_BDr._} 2.

[^28]: Perhaps the giantess Angrboða, mother by Loki of three monstrous children: Hel, Fenrir and Jǫrmungandr. But Loki himself has also been suggested, perhaps disguised as the giantess Þǫkk.

[^29]: Or perhaps ‘victorious’. Either way, the tone is sardonic.

[^30]: Perhaps emend {_er_} ‘when’ to {_unz_} ‘until’.

[^31]: Loki, whom the gods bound as punishment for instigating Baldr’s death, will escape his bonds at Ragnarok. Cf. {_Vsp._} 34, 49.

